[Music]  as covet 19 sucks up medical resources  people with hiv feel deprived of care  on world aids day activists say a  prolonged pandemic is deepening the  inequalities that have long driven the  hiv epidemic  the medical care the financing in the  hiv sector  all that no longer exists  what needs to be done to fight both  covet 19 and hiv  welcome to your covenant 19 special i'm  chris colbert berlin while much of the  world has been focusing on the  coronavirus pandemic the un is warning  on this world aids day that the fight  against covet 19 is seriously impeding  global efforts of ending  the disease by 2030 and those affected  are still subjected to stigma and  discrimination  frank  is hiv positive but with the treatment  available today he can lead a healthy  life  nevertheless his diagnosis in 2016 came  as a shock  my only thought was i'm going to die  even though hiv is all around us i never  thought i would be affected  thoughts no longer revolve around death  today's medication keeps him healthy but  he suffers from the stigma of prejudice  and exclusion  in the 1970s a virus was discovered  which is thought to have been  transmitted from monkeys to humans  over time it attacks and destroys the  immune system  panic spread across the world fueled by  the lack of aids awareness  in 1991 queen's lead singer freddie  mercury died of aids  a year later a tribute concert watched  by some 70 million people worldwide was  held in london  it was then that the new york-based  visual aids artist caucus made the red  ribbon the universal symbol of awareness  german for advice and action is the name  of this drop-in support center run by  the german aid service organization  frank shep got counseling here after his  positive hiv test  yet even today the organization's  counselors hear many stories about  discrimination  they've been mentioning people lose  their jobs because the name of a doctor  known to specialize in hiv is on their  medical certificate  they get asked why are you going to that  doctor  and they get fired  frank  pays regular visits to his doctor  so the effectiveness of his medication  can be monitored  thanks to medical advancements people  with hiv can now lead normal lives  of the people who are diagnosed these  days and receive antiretroviral therapy  that is hiv treatment over 95 percent  have a viral load below the detection  limit  when the load is at an undetectable  level they can't transmit it to another  person  i don't think people are generally aware  of this even doctors who work in  hospitals  there's a lot of discrimination against  people with hiv in the health care  system too  frank  wants to help fight prejudice and  educate people  he also founded a self-help group  no one should be afraid of people who  are hiv positive  because with the kind of treatment that  he receives  transmitting the virus to others is  impossible  you can live a normal life with hiv just  like frank chap  earlier i spoke to nigel garrett head of  vaccines and pathogenesis research at  the center  for the aids program of research in  south africa and i asked him if he  agreed with anthony fauci the u.s top  infectious disease expert who says that  the pandemic has diverted  scientific and financial resources from  the fight against aids  yeah i think that's certainly true uh  chris we we've seen it in uh in our  setting where i work in kwazulu-natal  in south africa where  uh the the number of tests being done on  people has reduced dramatically by about  50 percent  uh about half the people only started on  antiretroviral treatment which is  obviously vital for survival if you have  hiv  and then also on the research front uh  most of the research funding was now  channeled into cope uh into corbett  research and uh obviously accelerating  the vaccine pipeline in in  for sasko v2 so  i think overall um you know basically  the attention of all policy makers has  shifted to some degree to  to covet but we i think we're also  learning a lot about  obviously vaccines and possibilities  that we can back translate to hiv  hopefully once we've got this  pandemic under control  now  several case reports suggest that some  virus variants including omicron that  everybody's talking about now may have  evolved in  immunocompromised people potentially  hiv patients can you explain why  yeah so there have been two or three  case reports  one in the uk where they followed  someone who was on chemotherapy and  severely immunocompromised  and they just found persistent shedding  of of the virus  which then means that the immune  response is still there but it's it's  not able to clear the virus  which then allows the virus to mutate  in in a in a single human being  and a similar case was  also found in in south africa in an  hiv-positive patient who had a very low  immune system  uh not on antiretroviral treatment  and  they found that  this particular person developed three  or four  mutations  over a certain amount of months so  probably about a year  so my hypothesis is that this is kind of  a  what they call a reservoir for for  variant evolution  and i think we need to do a lot more  research  which larger implications would this  then have if there was a  link between the omicron variant and  hiv patients or immunocompromised  patients  yeah i think basically we need to push  vaccinations to everyone and  in a lot of countries now the us  uk and i think germany as well have  implemented a third booster dose for um  as part of the primary vaccination dose  uh for immunocompromised  patients  so i think that's that's crucial that we  keep pushing vaccinations and then  obviously for hiv specifically we need  to ensure that we test as many people as  possible and start people on treatment  to improve their immune system so that  we can avoid this phenomenon from  happening  why is hiv so much harder to fight  than covet 19.  um well there it's it's a very different  virus so there are many reasons some of  them are  biological of course and then some of  them are maybe more behavioral so hiv is  a chronic infection once you infect it  you  it stays with you  so often people are what we call  asymptomatic and they don't know that  they're infected  and then continue to infect  especially if they're not on treatment  and suppress from in terms of their  viral load  and then also there are some more  structural issues like stigma and  discrimination still  hiv is predominantly sexually  transmitted infection  so surrounding that you get a lot of  stigma evolving and then  obviously that does not help in terms of  the prevention effort and also the  treatment effort  and nigel if we focus on the medical  aspect aspect here um we spoke back in  june and what has changed since then  where does the field stand today  regarding a potential vaccination  against hiv  yeah so we had some exciting new  findings the uh i think i mentioned it  in june the imbecoda trial which was the  r26 so that's the  johnson johnson platform that's also  used in covet  they developed a mosaic vaccine  we have now got the results of that it  showed a 25 reduction in hiv  transmissions  unfortunately not enough of an effect to  show  statistical  significance but enough to do correlate  analyses so we we will be looking at you  know what potentially protected some of  the population  um  so that was one and then there's a  larger trial that is still ongoing in  the us and  south america in  [Music]  msm so menu of sex with men and  transgender people and we're hoping to  learn more about this concept  and then also the  amp studies so this antibody mediated  prevention trial was also presented this  year  and found that  these antibodies uh broadly neutralizing  antibodies essentially can  prevent infection with a sensitive virus  but not a resistant virus  so that is  where we are now heading with the field  in terms of giving combination antibody  bodies for prevention  nigel garrett of the center for the aids  program of research in south africa or  caprisun nigel thank you  thank you thanks for having me  the newly detected coronavirus variant  is also the topic of one of your  questions for our science correspondent  daryl williams  what do we know so far about the omicron  variant  both a lot and still very little a lot  because we already know a lot about sars  kovi ii and  although this variant has a long list of  mutations compared to earlier versions  of the virus  experts keep emphasizing that it isn't a  completely different pathogen it's the  old one with some new characteristics  and there are are three characteristics  in particular that are really  interesting for us first is  does it spread faster than other  variants  second is does it make you sicker than  other variants and third is  can it get around your immune defenses  easily even if you've been vaccinated  or recovered  it'll take weeks to accumulate enough  data to move  beyond speculation for any of those  three questions but but some early  indicators are leading some scientists  to make  educated guesses  for example um early data from the  variants spread in south africa where  omicron was initially identified  indicates that it could indeed be pretty  transmissible though  just how it stacks up to delta for  instance remains to be seen if it is  though the reasons will lie in its  genetic code scientists have detected  dozens  of new mutations in the rna  that encodes for omicron's spike protein  which is shown in pink in this picture  it's the key that the virus uses  to break into cells so  if the new variant does prove highly  transmissible the reason will likely lie  there  other mutations have led some  researchers to think that omicron could  also be better at getting around  immunity so  this might end up being the first  variant where we have to tailor  messenger rna vaccines but we'll only  know that in a couple of weeks on the  positive side  a few doctors in south africa have said  that patient symptoms and outbreaks  there  have remained mostly mild  especially in people who were vaccinated  but that's just anecdotal evidence from  a country with a young population and  and only studies that are racing ahead  now  will provide more hard data on whether  or not the variant  actually is more or less deadly so  watch the space i'm i'm sure i'm going  to be talking about omicron  plenty in the weeks to come  that's our show thanks for watching stay  safe  [Music]  you

